imes the trust takes  weeks or months  um obviously we we gain the trust of  General Sadat but you know there's a lot  of people around me around him so you  know making everyone else comfortable  with with our cameras  um at what really amounts to be the most  difficult moment in their lives  um  was tough and you know obviously as you  acknowledge like you know yes of course  we found ourselves in very dangerous and  difficult situations numerous times uh  countless times  um and making this film  from from the sort of obvious moments  like you know that was me filming in the  Blackhawk helicopter as we were getting  shot at by the Taliban as they're  shooting Rockets  um at the helicopter and you know in  those moments  you have zero agency like I don't know  how to fight helicopter I don't know how  to shoot a gun  um  all I can do is focus on the craft of  filmmaking on on my camera you know I  can't you know  control anything else so I choose to  focus on focusing on my exposure on  aperture on making sure the record  buttons on  um and that sort of calmed me down on  these very intense situations  um you know there's obvious moments like  that in the film then there's more you  know banal moments in the film like  yeah I mean there's there's a scene  obviously where  we narrowly miss a suicide attack to his  Convoy that you know I was driving in  with him  um but you know every other car ride  had this tension of knowing that the  Taliban were trying to take him out that  that they were sending suicide bombers  towards him or trying to at least every  single day so even just driving the car  was scary  um even though you might not feel that  within every frame uh while driving  the scene towards the end of the movie  of course you start with it and then you  end with it at the Kabul airports with  all the people scrambling to to try to  get on planes and get out of the country  uh it's heartbreaking we saw  bits and pieces of it on you know the  national news