 you know door that closes  the door opens with every every failure  comes an opportunity and the opportunity  that this gave us was  you know as we sort of pondered  at this point it was Tim and I shooting  you know what is the story we're telling  and it allowed us to open up the  aperture to show the civilians that  the green rays were fighting for that  General start was fighting for the  civilians that so often in these  discussions of Wars get lost  um  you know  big policy decisions are often made by  white men and white houses far away but  they impact families Sons daughters  mothers sisters and  it was important to humanize that and I  don't think the film would be what it  would be if we didn't have that side of  the quote-unquote story  um and so when we landed we made this  decision to leave the wire to leave the  airport and go out into the city to see  what you know Taliban rule felt like  um one of the first things we filmed was  that Taliban meeting  um which was obviously quite surreal  being on that stage uh  you know sneaking into that meeting be  on that stage after having spent most of  my conscience just adult life hearing  about the Taliban previous several  months being shot at by the Taliban  um and to be on that stage with them and  then we started to go around to the  different gates to film you know from a  civilian perspective you know this  desperate plea to flee  um you know the impending Taliban  takeover  and yeah I mean it was it was  just some of the toughest situations  I've ever found myself in my career that  you know the scene that you mentioned in  the question of  when you know a sea of people or or  thousands of people are pushing up  against the cement wall trying to get  through you know I made a decision to  make my way through that crowd and get  to the front of it and truly I mean  probably probably one of the scary  moments of my career there's a really  really high likelihood of a stampede  um there's tons of little 