at you can  um  or maybe both yeah it's at the moment  it's probably like what the hell am I  doing here  no I mean it's a you know there's a  there's a sort of PR answer that  question and there's a personal answer  to the question I  look I mean these films have taken a lot  out of me there's no question about that  um  I  I suffer you know from PTSD I have  nightmares often  um  you know the last two years have been  pretty heavy between making the first  wave of film about the first four months  of covid in New York or where I was  embedded in ICU and queens to this to  you know all the other films I've made  it's been heavy you know I've seen a lot  of death and it's  um  you know that's that's one side of  things and then the other side of things  is I'm extremely privileged you know I  have a blue passport that allows me to  come home that allows me to sit in my  office here in New York and talk to you  um and  you know with that privilege comes  responsibility and so I do feel a  responsibility to continue to make films  like this to tell stories that  um  you know I think are important to tell  and you know as documentary filmmakers I  feel like we're filling a void  in  in the sort of media landscape if you  will uh where you know as news budgets  are shrinking as foreign bureaus are  shrinking you know we have this also  privilege of of living with a story for  months if not years both in shooting and  editing  um and I guess on one basic level  retrograde is a historical document  of this moment in time and you know  hopefully something we can learn from  because  retrograding a war zone leaving a war  zone is something that has happened  throughout history and will continue to  happen as we fight Wars and what can we  learn from it  um so I'm just wondering what what's the  status of Sammy Sadat now uh at the end  of the movie he's in the UK is that  where he still is no  um he's still an exile he's not in  Afghanistan  um  and yeah I think it's he's having it's  it's it's tough it's tough  um  I guess that's probably an  understatement I mean it's he's having a  really tough time obviously and not only  did he  you know lose the war  um he's a refugee  um in another country  um and obviously all the  trauma of of of  what he and his men went through in  those final days and final weeks and  fighting  um so I think he's processing a lot  right now  um so my last question was going to be  what's next for you which is you know  interesting we're talking about you know  the we're just talking now about the  aftermath for you making your films  um but are you working on something else  um currently  yes I'm I'm editing a film I've shot for  the past several months about uh the  musician John Batiste uh and his wife  sheilaika  um  totally different type of film  um but something I'm really excited  about too so that sounds great this is  the first time you've made a music  subject film  um I did something a couple years ago on  uh J Balvin I called the boy from  Medellin  um so I've I've sort of  yeah done it once before but this is uh  is music one of your passions  I yeah I think for sure I mean I I  wouldn't even necessarily say this John  Phil I mean it is he's a musician and  there's a lot of music in the film but  it's I think it's a lot more than that I  hope  um  so  yeah  um so when does retrograde come out is  it this Friday  um in La it opens in theaters this  Friday yeah  okay it opened this past weekend in New  York and it's opening  um  uh in L.A and and expanding across the  country uh this coming weekend  well I want to say thank you so much for  coming to talk with us about this film  also thank you for making it  um  it's it's a film that made me want to do  a lot more reading and  discovery of things that  I haven't been paying attention to  um which I think is great I always think  that's positive if I see a film and then  I want to explore that subject so  that feels like a success and I feel  like a lot of people will feel that way  about the film as well  and it's a tough subject and I  appreciate you  continuing to tackle  um  things like this that don't have any  clear answers you know  well I I really appreciate it and  yeah I think  what you just said is is definitely one  of the goals of what I do is you know I  think  I like presenting stories without clear  answers or or clear takeaways maybe  that's the reality of life  um and especially something as complex  as the war in Afghanistan  um  at the very least I hope people walk  away you know carrying a little bit more  with a tiny bit more empathy and  curiosity of of you know what what  happened there and what continues to  happen there and  um so thank you for for saying that  sure well thanks so much for coming  today and um good luck to you  um on this film  and on future films  [Music]


